 ],
 "literal": "Gold-like āvirai fresh flowers closely strung / many-threaded garland palmyra-made spirited horse / ornamented bell sounding, having mounted, shame destroyed / great affliction internal pain increasingly to excel / \"Such a woman did this,\" saying, standing before / her, blame speaking, this town / having realized there, here to go is there a way for me?",
 "translation": "Having mounted the spirited horse made of palmyra stems, adorned with a garland of many threads strung with fresh, gold-like āvirai flowers, with its ornamented bell ringing, casting away my shame as my deep internal pain of great affliction increases day by day, I will stand before everyone. When this town speaks blame of her, saying, 'This is what she did to him,' and she realizes my state, is there any other way for me to go from here?",
 "modern": "பொன்னைப் போன்ற ஆவிரையின் புதிய மலர்களைக் கொண்டு தொடுத்த பல நூல்களையுடைய மாலையை அணிந்த, பனை மடலால் செய்த குதிரையின் மேல் ஏறி, கட்டிய மணி ஒலிக்க, நாணத்தை விடுத்து, நெஞ்சை வருத்தும் காம நோய் நாளுக்கு நாள் வளர, \"இவள் தான் எனக்கு இதனைச் செய்தாள்\" என்று ஊரார் முன்னிலையில் அவளது பழியைச் சொல்லும் இவ்வூரில், அவள் என் நிலையை உணர்ந்து கொள்ளுமாறு செய்வதற்கு இதைத் தவிர வேறு வழி எனக்கு உண்டோ?",
 "explanation": "The speaker is the hero, expressing his desperate state of love-sickness to his friend or himself. Unable to attain his beloved due to obstacles, he threatens to perform maṭalēṟutal, a public act of riding a mock horse made of jagged palmyra stems. He describes decorating this horse with yellow āvirai flowers and bells, discarding all sense of shame as his internal torment increases. By riding this horse through the streets, he intends to make the villagers blame the heroine for his misery. He believes that once she and her family realize the depth of his suffering through this public scandal, they will finally agree to the marriage, leaving him with no other path but this extreme step.",
